 the shims being consistent","ABSENCE: no statement of publish ordering or atomicity (e.g., write checkpoint LAST after both shims are in place, or swap via an atomic rename of a single release symlink)"],"finding":"The plan publishes two host shims (rebar, rebar-mcp) plus the last-good.sha checkpoint as a group, and the failure ACs require all of them to stay byte-identical on rejection while the no-op AC keys off last-good.sha — but the plan never specifies how this multi-artifact publish is made atomic or in what order, leaving a mid-publish interruption able to produce a half-published runtime (shims swapped but checkpoint stale, or one shim updated and not the other) that defeats the 'no change (already on <installed-sha>)' detection.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f6e36f91b2e56ae19","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach — 'only then publish both host shims and checkpoint SHA'; ACs for injected sync/smoke failures and the immediately-following no-op","norm_id":"n9ba72d0bdeaafbba","priority":0.5956,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["The updater is killed (launchd timeout, host reboot) after swapping the rebar shim but before rebar-mcp or before writing last-good.sha","A crash between shim swap and checkpoint write leaves shims pointing at the new runtime while last-good.sha still names the old SHA, so the next hourly run re-installs instead of logging a no-op"],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"Publish through a single atomic pointer swap: assemble the fully-synced release under a temp release dir, point both shims at it via one atomically-renamed release symlink, then write last-good.sha as the final step; on any interruption the previous release symlink and checkpoint remain the live, consistent state.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8889,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"medium"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Specify that the two shims plus checkpoint SHA are published atomically (e.g. write to temp names then rename, or update checkpoint last) so an interrupted publish cannot leave shims and last-good.sha inconsistent.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T12","T2","T4","T5b","T5d","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["T5b"],"decision":"advisory","drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'Build and smoke-test an unpublished candidate; only then publish both host shims and checkpoint SHA.'","The failure-probe ACs assert byte-identical prior state only for injected sync/smoke failures, not for a crash during the publish step itself."],"finding":"The publish step swaps two host shims plus the checkpoint SHA but does not specify atomicity or ordering for that multi-artifact commit; an interruption mid-publish (e.g. after the first shim is written but before the checkpoint) could leave the two shims and checkpoint inconsistent.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f9c710a00f9f86874","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach: 'only then publish both host shims and checkpoint SHA'","norm_id":"n64a8c8180d0de427","priority":0.5863,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["Updater process is killed after writing rebar shim but before rebar-mcp or l